WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.116 --> 00:00:02.997
- I love all the people
protesting Facebook on Instagram.

00:00:02.997 --> 00:00:06.485
We prove as humans all the
time how we think about privacy

00:00:06.485 --> 00:00:09.619
which is, we love to talk
about it, but we don't fear it

00:00:09.619 --> 00:00:11.844
as much, because the underlining issue is,

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:14.280
the amount of bad things
that happen to the health

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.390
and wellbeing of our family,
or having our money stolen

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:20.907
is extremely far and few between.

00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:22.719
- [Emily] You bought
Facebook stock on the back

00:00:22.719 --> 00:00:25.599
of this drop, how much
did you buy, and why?

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:27.943
- I bought a couple hundred
thousand dollars worth

00:00:27.943 --> 00:00:31.532
of shares, I bought
some more this morning.

00:00:31.532 --> 00:00:34.624
Because I'm a long-term believer
in the stock and I don't

00:00:34.624 --> 00:00:37.211
think there's gonna be a
lot of moments over the next

00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:40.907
three to four years, outside
of macroeconomics, that

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:43.891
I'm gonna be able to take advantage of it.

00:00:43.891 --> 00:00:46.717
I don't tend to buy a lot
of publicly traded stocks.

00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:51.140
My original Facebook came
pre-IPO but, when it got hit

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.577
when all the Cambridge
Analytica stuff happened

00:00:53.577 --> 00:00:56.928
and Mark was on trial I bought a ton.

00:00:56.928 --> 00:01:00.992
Then obviously off the missing
numbers or news recently,

00:01:00.992 --> 00:01:02.948
I've been buying up a little bit more.

00:01:02.948 --> 00:01:06.096
Mainly because I think in the
next four, seven, nine, 13, 26

00:01:06.096 --> 00:01:08.465
years it's a good buy.

00:01:08.465 --> 00:01:11.415
- So, it's interesting,
then that is a big bet,

00:01:11.415 --> 00:01:13.615
Roger McNmee, early Facebook
investor, who I'm sure

00:01:13.615 --> 00:01:14.588
you know has been out there

00:01:14.588 --> 00:01:16.787
very publicly critical of Facebook.

00:01:16.787 --> 00:01:19.433
He compared Facebook to
Phillip Morris to me,

00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:23.023
in terms of Facebook's
negative impact on the world.

00:01:23.023 --> 00:01:24.983
What about concerns about fake news?

00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:29.983
What about online hate? What
about concerns about privacy?

00:01:30.383 --> 00:01:34.377
- I think there is a stunning
non-conversation about

00:01:34.377 --> 00:01:38.710
taking on some sense of
responsibility, meaning sure

00:01:39.646 --> 00:01:44.088
but choosing CNN or Fox or MSNBC has

00:01:44.088 --> 00:01:47.604
as many dynamics as what
you're seeing in your feed, and

00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:51.968
then more poorly what you're
choosing to consume as real.

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:55.377
I mean to me the thought that
Facebook is any different

00:01:55.377 --> 00:01:59.502
than Twitter or Instagram,
which obviously they own,

00:01:59.502 --> 00:02:04.412
or websites or a million
different ways we consume

00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:09.129
is kind of laughable and
silly: I mean visa, and target,

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:12.731
and the American government
itself has had breaches

00:02:12.731 --> 00:02:15.341
of information far more dramatic.

00:02:15.341 --> 00:02:20.341
So, (clicks tongue) I
understand the concept,

00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:23.975
but I think making a
jump from Phillip Morris,

00:02:23.975 --> 00:02:27.082
which systematically
spent lobbying dollars

00:02:27.082 --> 00:02:30.561
and tried to pay off politicians
for four to five decades to

00:02:30.561 --> 00:02:34.861
suppress information, is an
awfully big jump by that person.

00:02:34.861 --> 00:02:38.113
- Okay, but what about
profitability versus privacy?

00:02:38.113 --> 00:02:40.101
Will one always trump the other?

00:02:40.101 --> 00:02:43.018
And can we actually trust Facebook?

00:02:45.136 --> 00:02:48.577
- I have no problem trusting
Facebook any more than

00:02:48.577 --> 00:02:51.810
I trust any other company on earth.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.612
Profitability and privacy
is kind of funny to me.

00:02:54.612 --> 00:02:58.045
We choose to do convenience
over profitability

00:02:58.045 --> 00:02:59.601
every day of the week.

00:02:59.601 --> 00:03:03.195
And privacy, I mean
the way we pick privacy

00:03:03.195 --> 00:03:06.198
on a pedestal is almost
nonexistent in our actions.

00:03:06.198 --> 00:03:08.883
Every person watching this
right now gives up privacy

00:03:08.883 --> 00:03:11.448
every day for convenience and speed.

00:03:11.448 --> 00:03:13.969
Whether it's gonna be Amazon,
Google, or Apple, or Facebook,

00:03:13.969 --> 00:03:16.272
or any of these companies
that sit as a layer on top

00:03:16.272 --> 00:03:19.233
of the internet, we have
shown through our actions

00:03:19.233 --> 00:03:23.142
over the last 30 years, and continue to,

00:03:23.142 --> 00:03:25.264
with the usage of Instagram and others.

00:03:25.264 --> 00:03:28.051
I love all the people protesting
Facebook on Instagram.

00:03:28.051 --> 00:03:31.460
We prove as humans all the time
how we think about privacy,

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.372
which is we love to talk
about it, but we don't fear

00:03:34.372 --> 00:03:37.111
it as much because the
underlining issue is the amount

00:03:37.111 --> 00:03:39.714
of bad things that happen
to the health and well-being

00:03:39.714 --> 00:03:42.228
of our family or having our money stolen

00:03:42.228 --> 00:03:45.404
is extremely far and few between.

00:03:45.404 --> 00:03:48.576
- What about the forces
that may be bigger than us?

00:03:48.576 --> 00:03:51.465
The fact that Russia tried
to hack our elections,

00:03:51.465 --> 00:03:54.341
using Facebook, and there's
evidence that they're doing

00:03:54.341 --> 00:03:57.126
it again to sew discord.

00:03:57.126 --> 00:03:58.494
Has Facebook done enough to

00:03:58.494 --> 00:04:01.577
prevent itself from being weaponized?

00:04:02.682 --> 00:04:06.073
- Facebook, this network,
Fox, CNN, Twitter,

00:04:06.073 --> 00:04:08.772
it's all the same game
where I think about it

00:04:08.772 --> 00:04:11.830
from a macro, which is we make decisions.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:14.456
Facebook or the Russians
didn't make us pull for Trump

00:04:14.456 --> 00:04:18.833
or Hillary, so, I think the
information that people are

00:04:18.833 --> 00:04:22.405
buying ads and running
them in our screens is

00:04:22.405 --> 00:04:25.018
very interesting because,
if you look at the execution

00:04:25.018 --> 00:04:28.178
of people that run political
ads, the cost of penetrating

00:04:28.178 --> 00:04:31.159
somebody with a different
opinion versus one that already

00:04:31.159 --> 00:04:33.822
reinforces your stance
is where this starts

00:04:33.822 --> 00:04:36.582
getting far more interesting.

00:04:36.582 --> 00:04:40.859
I think this is one of the
great examples in human time

00:04:40.859 --> 00:04:43.706
of us not interested in accountability.

00:04:43.706 --> 00:04:45.636
Facebook didn't make you a racist,

00:04:45.636 --> 00:04:47.516
you made yourself a racist.

00:04:47.516 --> 00:04:48.872
- [Emily] So let's talk about Twitter.

00:04:48.872 --> 00:04:53.872
Because you use Twitter a
lot, we saw the same huge dip

00:04:53.936 --> 00:04:56.497
on the back of Twitter
earnings and it's very clear

00:04:56.497 --> 00:05:00.025
that Twitter is just not
in a high growth state.

00:05:00.025 --> 00:05:01.970
They've hit a plateau,
in fact they actually

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.873
lost users over the last quarter.

00:05:03.873 --> 00:05:06.799
The influences there, the
President of the United States

00:05:06.799 --> 00:05:11.458
is using it as his megaphone
but it's not the next Facebook.

00:05:11.458 --> 00:05:13.782
- No, but it was never the next Facebook

00:05:13.782 --> 00:05:18.782
and the truth is Facebook
is pfft, all encompassing

00:05:18.864 --> 00:05:21.741
with Instagram and who knows
what Mark and Cheryl and

00:05:21.741 --> 00:05:23.285
the team is up to on their next M &amp; A

00:05:23.285 --> 00:05:25.254
or innovation gotwhats app.

00:05:25.254 --> 00:05:28.230
It is not the next Facebook,
I assume it's not priced

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.252
on wall street in the same manner.

00:05:30.252 --> 00:05:33.321
I will say this, if you look
very closely to Moon Pie,

00:05:33.321 --> 00:05:37.677
which is a small snack brand,
if you look at Wendy's,

00:05:37.677 --> 00:05:41.463
I will say the business world
is starting to understand

00:05:41.463 --> 00:05:45.050
how to use Facebook to drive business.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:47.611
We're working with Kraft
Heinz on Miracle Whip.

00:05:47.611 --> 00:05:51.514
It stuns me how much
Twitter is the back bone

00:05:51.514 --> 00:05:54.406
of driving sales at
Albertson's and Wal Mart.

00:05:54.406 --> 00:05:59.406
So I will say that maybe
the width of attention

00:06:00.051 --> 00:06:04.624
on Twitter is not growing to a
stock market's want and needs

00:06:04.624 --> 00:06:07.240
but, the depth of
attention in the platform

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.393
and the ways to maneuver
in it and it's ad product.

00:06:10.393 --> 00:06:12.899
I've been, over the last
three to four years,

00:06:12.899 --> 00:06:16.965
quite critical and not excited
about Twitter publicly.

00:06:16.965 --> 00:06:20.794
But I no question have seen
over the last six to 12 months,

00:06:20.794 --> 00:06:24.875
a resurgence different
than the Trump effect

00:06:24.875 --> 00:06:28.816
of just how much attention
is in the platform.

00:06:28.816 --> 00:06:29.690
When you have attention I think

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:32.612
you have the ultimate currency.

00:06:32.612 --> 00:06:34.931
It will be interesting to see
what they do with their OTT.

00:06:34.931 --> 00:06:36.672
I think they should play in that space.

00:06:36.672 --> 00:06:38.600
I think they were flirting
with that a year or two ago.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.931
Again, I don't know how
earnings are done or how much

00:06:41.931 --> 00:06:45.073
the street values things,
but I will say depth,

00:06:45.073 --> 00:06:48.895
maybe not width, is emerging on Twitter.

00:06:48.895 --> 00:06:50.612
- [Emily] The top five
companies by market cap are

00:06:50.612 --> 00:06:54.228
Facebook, Apple, Amazon,
Google, Microsoft.

00:06:54.228 --> 00:06:56.863
In 2007 just before the financial crisis,

00:06:56.863 --> 00:07:00.350
one of them was a tech company
and that was Microsoft.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:04.106
Is the market putting too
many eggs in the tech basket?

00:07:04.106 --> 00:07:06.865
- Honestly, I want to be
very fair for the audience,

00:07:06.865 --> 00:07:09.026
I think about things in such long term.

00:07:09.026 --> 00:07:12.480
The thought of me understanding
the short term economics

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:15.367
of 90 day terms and numbers
that most of the viewers here

00:07:15.367 --> 00:07:20.035
play on and the arbitrary
nature, and the fact that we're

00:07:20.035 --> 00:07:21.549
playing financial engineering,
not actually building

00:07:21.549 --> 00:07:24.276
businesses, in the macro, of course not.

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:25.600
We're not going backwards.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:28.072
Tech companies will
continue to gain momentum.

00:07:28.072 --> 00:07:30.925
Let me pull it out here, this
is where we actually live,

00:07:30.925 --> 00:07:34.776
not here, so in the super long term, no.

00:07:34.776 --> 00:07:38.566
In the short term, I have
no sense if the market

00:07:38.566 --> 00:07:41.562
has overpriced these companies based on

00:07:41.562 --> 00:07:46.176
what the numbers they can
hit over the next 90 days.

00:07:46.176 --> 00:07:47.878
People aren't gonna be
watching ABC and NBC

00:07:47.878 --> 00:07:49.398
over Netflix, right?

00:07:49.398 --> 00:07:51.159
People are not gonna
be consuming newspapers

00:07:51.159 --> 00:07:53.863
over Facebook, that is
only gonna continue.

00:07:53.863 --> 00:07:56.024
We're just starting in that world.

00:07:56.024 --> 00:07:58.403
Short term economics and
the way the street likes

00:07:58.403 --> 00:08:01.090
to play, that's up to all of you guys.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:05.673
The danger in a five, 10, 15
year macro, I don't see it.

00:08:05.673 --> 00:08:08.034
The reality is we're only
heading in one direction,

00:08:08.034 --> 00:08:10.556
which is technology doesn't
care about our feelings

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:13.334
or our short term
judgement, or Wall Street.

00:08:13.334 --> 00:08:14.991
It just continues to advance

00:08:14.991 --> 00:08:18.301
and humans are engaging
deeper and deeper and deeper

00:08:18.301 --> 00:08:21.430
and will continue to in perpetuity.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:22.684
- Let's talk about the long term when

00:08:22.684 --> 00:08:24.574
it comes to Apple and Netflix.

00:08:24.574 --> 00:08:27.021
You played a role on
an Apple original show.

00:08:27.021 --> 00:08:31.223
That show unfortunately didn't last long.

00:08:31.223 --> 00:08:35.223
Certainly not because of you, but Apple is

00:08:36.208 --> 00:08:38.574
doing this original
content grand experiment.

00:08:38.574 --> 00:08:41.597
Do you think they can
succeed where Netflix has

00:08:41.597 --> 00:08:44.531
when they haven't shown an
ability to do so thus far?

00:08:44.531 --> 00:08:46.507
- I think the sheer amount
of money that I'm hearing

00:08:46.507 --> 00:08:49.480
they're throwing at it
makes me optimistic.

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:52.179
I spend a good amount of time in Hollywood

00:08:52.179 --> 00:08:54.254
and Silicon Valley and Madison Avenue.

00:08:54.254 --> 00:08:56.790
You get a real sense, I
think the sense, if you talk

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:59.084
to any of your Hollywood
friends, production

00:08:59.084 --> 00:09:00.984
and agents and things of that nature,

00:09:00.984 --> 00:09:03.624
is everybody understands
that it's an Amazon, Apple,

00:09:03.624 --> 00:09:07.556
Netflix, Hulu, and who's
the next OTT, Facebook,

00:09:07.556 --> 00:09:12.051
kind of world, that the
networks are in deep trouble.

00:09:12.051 --> 00:09:15.130
Even the cable providers,
even with mergers, the

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:18.411
sheer dollars that these
internet companies are gonna

00:09:18.411 --> 00:09:21.207
throw at content, the NFL rights.

00:09:21.207 --> 00:09:24.563
It's gonna be an Amazon,
Google, Facebook, Apple world.

00:09:24.563 --> 00:09:28.709
And then, kind of
fighting for that, so yes.

00:09:28.709 --> 00:09:33.430
I think that programming the
IP is what matters in the end.

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:36.624
When Apple, not if, has its Breaking Bad,

00:09:36.624 --> 00:09:40.101
or Orange is the New Black, or NFL,

00:09:40.101 --> 00:09:43.853
that it will suck people
in because of the IP.

00:09:43.853 --> 00:09:45.584
It's the same way that
so many more people watch

00:09:45.584 --> 00:09:48.598
Hulu and Amazon than a lot
of people thought they would

00:09:48.598 --> 00:09:52.098
24 months ago, predicated on just one hit.

00:09:53.645 --> 00:09:55.931
- I know you say you're not
an expert on short term market

00:09:55.931 --> 00:09:58.261
dynamics but we're all
watching Apple and Amazon

00:09:58.261 --> 00:09:59.578
in this race to a trillion dollars,

00:09:59.578 --> 00:10:02.019
Amazon sort of coming from behind.

00:10:02.019 --> 00:10:03.292
In the big picture do you see more

00:10:03.292 --> 00:10:05.018
potential in one company or the other?

00:10:05.018 --> 00:10:07.073
- In a substantial way.

00:10:07.073 --> 00:10:09.722
I think the Delta between
Jeff Bezos and Tim Cook

00:10:09.722 --> 00:10:14.577
as a 2018 CEO is unbelievably substantial.

00:10:14.577 --> 00:10:17.336
I would bet every day
of the week on Amazon.

00:10:17.336 --> 00:10:19.516
- [Emily] Why?

00:10:19.516 --> 00:10:22.132
- Because I think Jeff has
just shown so much more ability

00:10:22.132 --> 00:10:25.161
to navigate the end consumer's attention,

00:10:25.161 --> 00:10:29.188
which I think is the
ultimate value in society.

00:10:29.188 --> 00:10:32.943
I think that Tim A. Is an incredible guy

00:10:32.943 --> 00:10:34.927
and I like a lot of the
things he as a human

00:10:34.927 --> 00:10:37.941
and the company is trying
to push to stand for.

00:10:37.941 --> 00:10:41.233
But he's been the CEO now
what, seven, eight years.

00:10:41.233 --> 00:10:43.921
I'm not sure you can
help me with this but,

00:10:43.921 --> 00:10:46.009
I mean what, ear pods?

00:10:46.009 --> 00:10:47.988
I have not seen Apple win in voice

00:10:47.988 --> 00:10:51.357
the way they should have with
the lead they had with Siri.

00:10:51.357 --> 00:10:53.133
The home pod's still not out there.

00:10:53.133 --> 00:10:55.235
We have not seen them
win an original program.

00:10:55.235 --> 00:10:58.468
It's now been 24, 36 months
that cat's been out of the bag.

00:10:58.468 --> 00:11:01.434
There's been no hardware
innovation outside the ear pods

00:11:01.434 --> 00:11:03.698
that has any blimp of anything.

00:11:03.698 --> 00:11:06.608
All they've really done
is slowly advance things

00:11:06.608 --> 00:11:09.496
that have been in place prior.

00:11:09.496 --> 00:11:11.681
You know, at least the way
I think about the world,

00:11:11.681 --> 00:11:14.218
I don't look at it the numbers
and the ways short term

00:11:14.218 --> 00:11:15.825
and maximizing profit every 90 days,

00:11:15.825 --> 00:11:17.548
the way everybody who watches this does.

00:11:17.548 --> 00:11:20.642
In the long term, what
it means for consumers,

00:11:20.642 --> 00:11:23.522
Amazon through a magnitude of things

00:11:23.522 --> 00:11:26.939
has done a far better job of
mattering to the end consumer

00:11:26.939 --> 00:11:29.065
and continues to gain leverage.

00:11:29.065 --> 00:11:31.118
- You mention the Networks are in trouble.

00:11:31.118 --> 00:11:34.023
I have to ask about CBS,
which is currently deciding

00:11:34.023 --> 00:11:37.335
the fate of Les Moonves
after these accusations

00:11:37.335 --> 00:11:41.376
from a New Yorker article about
crossing a line with women.

00:11:41.376 --> 00:11:42.648
Should he stay or go?

00:11:42.648 --> 00:11:45.195
- Well if he's guilty he should go.

00:11:45.195 --> 00:11:48.936
- Even after such a long
and successful track record?

00:11:48.936 --> 00:11:51.585
- Doing the wrong thing is
always the wrong thing Emily.

00:11:51.585 --> 00:11:54.550
You're only as good as your
last at bat or the truth.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:57.694
We now don't look at Cristopher
Columbus the way we used to

00:11:57.694 --> 00:12:01.232
after we looked carefully
in all the things he did.

00:12:01.232 --> 00:12:03.664
So of course.

00:12:03.664 --> 00:12:06.412
I'm very aware, listen I
love winning in business too,

00:12:06.412 --> 00:12:09.171
everybody who's watching does,
but if you're a human being

00:12:09.171 --> 00:12:11.895
that's willing to look
the other way for money,

00:12:11.895 --> 00:12:13.898
you're not a good human being.

00:12:13.898 --> 00:12:16.685
- So Gary, you've launched
a new media mens-lifestyle

00:12:16.685 --> 00:12:18.154
grand focused on the intersection

00:12:18.154 --> 00:12:21.220
of entrepreneurship and pop-culture.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:22.523
You say there's not gonna be any walls.

00:12:22.523 --> 00:12:25.956
There are no walls between
editorial and advertising.

00:12:25.956 --> 00:12:27.728
What's the vision here?

00:12:27.728 --> 00:12:30.289
- 18 months ago I bought purewow.com.

00:12:30.289 --> 00:12:32.914
I have VaynorMedia, the
holding company's VaynorX,

00:12:32.914 --> 00:12:35.954
VaynorMedia's the agency,
Madison Avenue madmen.

00:12:35.954 --> 00:12:40.187
Purewow and 137 PM is the publishing side,

00:12:40.187 --> 00:12:43.004
the hearse side of things.

00:12:43.004 --> 00:12:46.326
To me, if you really look
at what the BuzzFeeds

00:12:46.326 --> 00:12:49.957
and the vices and the refinery
29s did a half decade ago

00:12:49.957 --> 00:12:51.904
is they kind of created publishing 2.0

00:12:51.904 --> 00:12:54.116
where they started to do creative work

00:12:54.116 --> 00:12:56.811
for clients instead of
just buying commercials

00:12:56.811 --> 00:12:58.982
on TV networks or in magazines.

00:12:58.982 --> 00:13:02.248
The branded content world
started to emerge, and you saw

00:13:02.248 --> 00:13:04.918
those crazy evaluations for
a lot of those companies.

00:13:04.918 --> 00:13:07.300
I think the continued
evolution of making content

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:09.398
and getting people to see it contextual,

00:13:09.398 --> 00:13:12.334
that brings the Proctor &amp;
Gambles and the Coca-Cola's value

00:13:12.334 --> 00:13:14.530
is what's going to happen in publishing.

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:18.388
We continue to try to innovate
on creating original videos

00:13:18.388 --> 00:13:21.279
and apps and event marketing.

00:13:21.279 --> 00:13:26.279
Things to help clients sell
beer and makeup and deodorant.

00:13:26.416 --> 00:13:30.116
- Last question, how are
all of these dynamics

00:13:30.116 --> 00:13:31.122
